@@ -0,0 +1,16 @@
+# HAL Engine ws test support
+
+| Field  | Value                |
+| ------ | -------------------- |
+| Issue  | re-cinq/HALEngine#93 |
+| Status | Shipped              |
+
+`src/transport/wsTestSupport.ts` is the harness the transport suites share when the engine has to be exercised over a real socket, which no test double can stand in for: `startEngine` binds an engine to an ephemeral port and returns the URL a client opens, `connectClient` opens a client the shared teardown closes whether its test passed or failed, `framesUntil` resolves with every frame type seen up to the one the test names, and `stopEngines` terminates every socket and server the suite opened. Each client records the error its socket reports, so a transport failure has somewhere to go. CI struck `awaitedSessionCreate.test.ts` hanging to the suite timeout because a server answered its client's upgrade with HTTP 404: the `ws` client reported the failure on its `error` event, nothing read that event, and the test awaiting frames sat out the whole timeout before the error escaped unhandled. The suite learned nothing it could report, and the worker sat idle for the timeout on top of it.
+
+## When the transport fails
+
+- A transport failure on a test client's socket — the refused upgrade above chief among them — reaches the awaiting `framesUntil` promise as a rejection carrying the handshake error the client received, so the test reports the real cause instead of hanging to the suite timeout and leaking an unhandled `error` event ([validated by: rejects the awaiting frames promise with the handshake error the client received](../../src/transport/wsTestSupport.test.ts#L31)).
+
+## Out of scope
+
+What the engine itself answers a refused upgrade with — the 401 of a failed authentication, the subprotocol-marker deprecation path — is server behaviour, owned by those specs. This one covers only what the shared harness guarantees to the suites that use it.
